<ansi fg="black-bold">.:</ansi> <ansi fg="magenta">Help for </ansi><ansi fg="command">strategy</ansi>

Once a battle starts it plays out on its own: nothing you type changes it
but a new company focus (<ansi fg="command">help tactics</ansi>).
What it plays out by is each character's strategy, set beforehand: yours,
and each companion's. A strategy is a role (what they do) and a target rule
(whom they go for).

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">Roles</ansi>

  <ansi fg="command">fighter</ansi>   fights with weapon or fists, and never casts.
  <ansi fg="command">healer</ansi>    heals anyone in your company below the healing threshold (half
            health, unless your company tactics say otherwise): a group heal
            when two or more are, else a single heal on the most hurt.
            With no one that hurt, or no mana, fights.
  <ansi fg="command">caster</ansi>    casts an attack spell every round it can pay for one: an area
            spell when two or more foes stand, else a single-target spell.
            Out of mana, fights.
  <ansi fg="command">guardian</ansi>  fights, and steps in front of its ward to take a blow meant
            for them: 2 guards a battle, one back every 2 rounds. Its
            ward is a member you name, else whoever is most hurt, within
            one column of it (<ansi fg="command">help guardian</ansi>).

Whatever its role, a warrior, rogue, or ranger also uses its class
ability on its own when the moment comes: a tackle, an opening strike, or
an aimed shot (<ansi fg="command">help abilities</ansi>).

Each character starts with its archetype's role: clerics heal, wizards
cast, witches hex (<ansi fg="command">help hexes</ansi>), everyone else fights. Nobody starts as a guardian. A healer or
caster casts only the spells it knows. Wizards start with Magic Missile and clerics with Minor Heal; a
companion knows its archetype's spells for its level (a cleric learns
Minor Heal All at level 3, a wizard Shower of Sparks, as players do).

Spells in a battle are real casts: they cost mana and take their chanting
rounds, but a spell the caster knows never fizzles. A weapon blow that draws blood on the caster
may break the chant, with half the mana back (<ansi fg="command">help interrupts</ansi>), so keep
healers and casters out of reach. Two healers never heal the same member
at once: one heal being chanted covers its patient. After a spell, the caster turns back to
the foe it was fighting. Mana doesn't come back on its own, in a battle or
out of one: only a camp rest, an inn or a draught refills it (<ansi fg="command">help mana</ansi>). A healer
heals you even when you are down and bleeding out.

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">Target rules</ansi>

  <ansi fg="command">weakest</ansi>    the foe with the least health left (the default)
  <ansi fg="command">strongest</ansi>  the foe with the most health left
  <ansi fg="command">wounded</ansi>    the most badly hurt foe, to finish it
  <ansi fg="command">nearest</ansi>    the front-most foe
  <ansi fg="command">furthest</ansi>   the back-most foe
  <ansi fg="command">leader</ansi>     their leader, the toughest of them
  <ansi fg="command">casters</ansi>    their spell-casters, one chanting a spell first
  <ansi fg="command">assist</ansi>     whatever you are striking (companions only)
  <ansi fg="command">defend</ansi>     the foe striking whichever of you is most hurt

A rule chooses among the foes a character can reach from its place in the
formation (<ansi fg="command">help formation</ansi>). When the rule's choice is out of reach, it
goes for the nearest foe it can reach instead; with none in reach at all,
it goes for the front-most foe (a blow at the back is caught by the front).
A spell reaches anyone, so a
caster's spell goes to the rule's choice wherever it stands.

A character keeps its target until that foe falls or can't be reached,
then picks again by its rule. <ansi fg="command">assist</ansi> and <ansi fg="command">defend</ansi> follow what moves: they
turn with your target, or to whoever strikes your most hurt member.

A company focus (<ansi fg="command">help tactics</ansi>) overrides every target rule here while
it is set: everyone goes for the focus's choice, and roles stay. It is also
the one thing you can change in a battle.

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">Commands</ansi>

  <ansi fg="command">strategy</ansi>                    how everyone in your company fights
  <ansi fg="command">strategy [who]</ansi>              one character, with the spells they'd cast
  <ansi fg="command">strategy [who] [role]</ansi>       set a role, e.g. <ansi fg="command">strategy dain healer</ansi>
  <ansi fg="command">strategy [who] target [rule]</ansi>
                              set a rule, e.g. <ansi fg="command">strategy tamsin target leader</ansi>
  <ansi fg="command">strategy [who] guard [other]</ansi>
                              a guardian of [other], e.g. <ansi fg="command">strategy tamsin guard me</ansi>;
                              leave out [other] to guard the most hurt
  <ansi fg="command">strategy [who] abilities off</ansi>
                              use no class abilities (<ansi fg="command">on</ansi> again; <ansi fg="command">help abilities</ansi>)
  <ansi fg="command">strategy [who] reserve [percent]</ansi>
                              keep that much mana (0 to 90) back from
                              attack spells; heals and hexes ignore it
  <ansi fg="command">strategy [who] default</ansi>      back to the archetype's default

[who] is <ansi fg="command">me</ansi>, or a companion by name (part of it will do) or number
(<ansi fg="command">#2</ansi>). The word target can be left out: <ansi fg="command">strategy tamsin leader</ansi>.
The word guard makes a guardian; to go for the foe striking your most
hurt, use <ansi fg="command">defend</ansi> (or protect).

Set strategies before a battle: during one they can be read but not
changed (only the company focus can be called: <ansi fg="command">help tactics</ansi>). Strategies are kept when you log out.

In the web client, the Combat tab shows how everyone fights; click a
member to change it (<ansi fg="command">help webclient</ansi>).

See also: <ansi fg="command">help abilities</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help guardian</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help tactics</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help combat</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help targeting</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help formation</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help cast</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help mana</ansi>.
